INSERT INTO lessons (id, topic_id, title, content, summary, difficulty, source_type, is_active, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT 'b3b3b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3b3b3b313', 'b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b213',
    'Banker''s Discount Concepts',
    '<h5><strong>1. CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATION</strong></h5><h5><br></h5><p>The concept of <strong>Banker’s Discount (BD)</strong> is used in commercial arithmetic and banking to calculate the present worth of a bill before its due date. When a bill of exchange is discounted by a bank before maturity, the bank deducts interest on the face value for the remaining time and pays the rest to the holder.</p><p>To understand Banker’s Discount, it is essential to recall:</p><ul><li><strong>True Discount (TD)</strong> — the difference between the face value of a bill and its present worth.</li><li><strong>Banker’s Discount (BD)</strong> — the interest on the face value of the bill for the period before its maturity.</li><li><strong>Banker’s Gain (BG)</strong> — the difference between the Banker’s Discount and True Discount.</li></ul><h5><br></h5><h5><strong>2. CORE THEORY &amp; PRINCIPLES</strong></h5><h5><br></h5><ol><li><strong>Face Value (F):</strong></li><li>The amount mentioned in the bill, payable at the end of the period.</li><li><strong>Present Worth (P):</strong></li><li>The value of the bill at present (after deducting the discount).</li><li><strong>Banker’s Discount (BD):</strong></li><li>The interest on the <strong>Face Value</strong> for the time between the date of discounting and the date of maturity.</li><li>$\text{BD} = \frac{F \times R \times T}{100}$</li><li><strong>True Discount (TD):</strong></li><li>The difference between the Face Value and its Present Worth.</li><li>$\text{TD} = \frac{F \times R \times T}{100 + R \times T}$</li><li><strong>Banker’s Gain (BG):</strong></li><li>The excess of Banker’s Discount over True Discount.</li><li>$\text{BG} = \text{BD} - \text{TD}$</li><li><strong>Relation between BD, TD, and BG:</strong></li><li>$\text{BG} = \frac{\text{TD}^2}{F}$</li></ol><h5><br></h5><h5><strong>3. MAGIC SHORTCUTS</strong></h5><h5><br></h5><ol><li>$\text{BD} = \frac{F \times R \times T}{100}$</li><li>$\text{TD} = \frac{F \times R \times T}{100 + R \times T}$</li><li>$\text{BG} = \frac{\text{TD}^2}{F}$</li><li>$\text{P} = F - \text{BD}$</li><li>$\text{F} = \text{P} + \text{TD}$</li></ol><p><br></p><h5><br></h5><h5><strong>4. ESSENTIAL FORMULAE &amp; METHODS</strong></h5><table table_id="eku3winlzp" id="eku3winlzp" class=""><tr row_id="ixcq3a0u8xc" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="ixcq3a0u8xc" cell_id="a6mcyxhjf7h" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p class="ql-align-center"><strong style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Concept</strong></p></td><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="ixcq3a0u8xc" cell_id="l2kzy3pzmy" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p class="ql-align-center"><strong style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Formula</strong></p></td><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="ixcq3a0u8xc" cell_id="pjjcboauik" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p class="ql-align-center"><strong style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Description</strong></p></td></tr><tr row_id="mtytwb7sglr" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="mtytwb7sglr" cell_id="wwe8788u87g" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Banker’s Discount (BD)</span></p></td><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="mtytwb7sglr" cell_id="apqsdkc9s4q" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">$\frac{F \times R \times T}{100}$</span></p></td><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="mtytwb7sglr" cell_id="b37j4rptdwq" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Interest on the face value for unexpired time</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="w4dpyebgzf" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="w4dpyebgzf" cell_id="kbecm7jryxm" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">True Discount (TD)</span></p></td><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="w4dpyebgzf" cell_id="u565o0iwkm" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">$\frac{F \times R \times T}{100 + R \times T}$</span></p></td><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="w4dpyebgzf" cell_id="q0aiy1tbaj8" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Difference between face value and present worth</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="3m4th0bdanq" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="3m4th0bdanq" cell_id="69t0p6uzm8q" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Banker’s Gain (BG)</span></p></td><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="3m4th0bdanq" cell_id="bm8i01c8nxj" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">$\frac{\text{TD}^2}{F}$</span></p></td><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="3m4th0bdanq" cell_id="25m0meqaq5li" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Difference between BD and TD</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="gsky2ykajpr" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="gsky2ykajpr" cell_id="iwzxphkocb" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Relation</span></p></td><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="gsky2ykajpr" cell_id="c05c7ru0lg8" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">$\text{BD} = \text{TD} + \text{BG}$</span></p></td><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="gsky2ykajpr" cell_id="5qmlr36n6f8" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Basic relationship</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="cm5qvlexnr" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="cm5qvlexnr" cell_id="ujtvl5u8kxg" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Present Worth (P)</span></p></td><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="cm5qvlexnr" cell_id="nqg2j3m0obr" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">$\frac{F \times 100}{100 + R \times T}$</span></p></td><td table_id="eku3winlzp" row_id="cm5qvlexnr" cell_id="bby5lma89pt" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Present value of bill</span></p></td></tr></table><p><br></p><h5><br></h5><h5><strong>5. PROBLEM RECOGNITION &amp; CLASSIFICATION</strong></h5><ol><li><strong>Type 1:</strong> Find Banker’s Discount and Banker’s Gain given Face Value, Rate, and Time.</li><li><strong>Type 2:</strong> Find Present Worth and True Discount.</li><li><strong>Type 3:</strong> Given BD and BG, find TD or Face Value.</li><li><strong>Type 4:</strong> Compare BD and TD over different bills.</li><li><strong>Type 5:</strong> Practical problems involving dates and maturity periods.</li></ol><h5><br></h5><h5><strong>6. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION METHODS</strong></h5><p><br></p><h6><strong>Example 1</strong></h6><p><strong>Question:</strong></p><p>Find the Banker’s Discount and Banker’s Gain on a bill of ₹6000 due in 6 months at 10% per annum.</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p>Given:</p><p>$F = 6000$, $R = 10%$, $T = \frac{1}{2}$ year</p><p>$\text{BD} = \frac{6000 \times 10 \times 1/2}{100} = 300$</p><p>$\text{TD} = \frac{6000 \times 10 \times 1/2}{100 + 10 \times 1/2} = \frac{30000}{105} \approx 285.71$</p><p>$\text{BG} = \text{BD} - \text{TD} = 300 - 285.71 = 14.29$</p><p><strong>Final Answers:</strong></p><p>Banker’s Discount = ₹300</p><p>Banker’s Gain = ₹14.29</p><p><br></p><h6><strong>Example 2</strong></h6><p><strong>Question:</strong></p><p>The Banker’s Discount on a certain bill for 4 months at 6% is ₹40. Find the True Discount and Banker’s Gain.</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p>$\text{BD} = 40$, $R = 6%$, $T = \frac{1}{3}$ year</p><p>We know $\text{BG} = \frac{\text{TD}^2}{F}$ and $\text{BD} = \text{TD} + \text{BG}$.</p><p>Using formula:</p><p>$\text{TD} = \frac{\text{BD} \times 100}{100 + R \times T}$</p><p>$\text{TD} = \frac{40 \times 100}{100 + 6 \times \frac{1}{3}} = \frac{4000}{102} \approx 39.22$</p><p>$\text{BG} = 40 - 39.22 = 0.78$</p><p><strong>Final Answers:</strong></p><p>True Discount = ₹39.22</p><p>Banker’s Gain = ₹0.78</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h5><strong>7. CONCLUSION</strong></h5><ul><li><strong>Banker’s Discount</strong> always exceeds <strong>True Discount</strong> by the <strong>Banker’s Gain</strong>.</li><li>The difference between the two discounts is small but significant in financial calculations.</li><li>Understanding the interrelation of BD, TD, and BG helps in solving banking and commercial arithmetic problems efficiently.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><strong>Practice Question:</strong></p><p>Find the Banker’s Discount, True Discount, and Banker’s Gain on a bill of ₹8,000 due in 9 months at 12% per annum.</p>',
    NULL, 'MEDIUM', 'CURATED', TRUE,
    CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP FROM DUAL
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE id = 'b3b3b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3b3b3b313');

-- 14. Surds & Indices -> topic b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b214
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, unit_id, name, description, difficulty, display_order, is_active, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT 'b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b214', '55555555-5555-5555-5555-555555555501',
    'b1b1b1b1-b1b1-b1b1-b1b1-b1b1b1b1b101', 'Surds & Indices',
    'Covers laws of exponents, simplification of radical expressions, and rationalizing denominators', 'MEDIUM', 42, TRUE,
    CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP FROM DUAL
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM topics WHERE id = 'b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b214');
INSERT INTO lessons (id, topic_id, title, content, summary, difficulty, source_type, is_active, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT 'b3b3b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3b3b3b314', 'b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b214',
    'Surds & Indices Concepts',
    '<h4>1. CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATION</h4><p>The topic of <em>Surds and Indices</em> forms the foundation for handling irrational numbers and exponential expressions efficiently.</p><ul><li><strong>Indices</strong> deal with powers and exponents — a compact way of expressing repeated multiplication.</li><li><strong>Surds</strong> deal with irrational numbers that cannot be simplified into rational form, often involving square roots, cube roots, etc.</li></ul><p>These two concepts together enable students to simplify large expressions, rationalize denominators, and solve algebraic equations involving roots and powers.</p><p><strong>Real-Life Relevance:</strong></p><ul><li>Used in physics (laws of motion, growth/decay), finance (compound interest, exponential growth), and engineering (wave equations, scaling models).</li><li>Also vital in aptitude tests for simplifying complex numbers, cube/square-based equations, and pattern-based reasoning.</li></ul><p><br></p><h4>2. CORE THEORY &amp; PRINCIPLES</h4><p><strong>A. INDICES (EXPONENTS)</strong></p><ol><li>If ‘a’ is a non-zero real number and ‘n’ is a positive integer, then:</li><li>aⁿ = a × a × a × … × a (n times).</li><li>Laws of Indices:</li></ol><ul><li>aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿaᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ(aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ(ab)ⁿ = aⁿbⁿ(a/b)ⁿ = aⁿ / bⁿa⁰ = 1 (if a ≠ 0)a⁻ⁿ = 1 / aⁿ</li></ul><p><strong>B. SURDS</strong></p><ol><li>A <strong>surd</strong> is an irrational root that cannot be simplified into a rational number.</li><li>Examples: √2, √3, ³√5, √7, etc.</li><li><strong>Order</strong> of a surd: If n√a is a surd, then its order is <em>n</em>.</li><li><strong>Simplest form of a surd:</strong></li></ol><ul><li>Factorize the number inside the root into perfect powers and residual parts.Example: √72 = √(36 × 2) = 6√2.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Rationalization:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class="ql-indent-1">Process of removing a surd from the denominator.Example: 1 / √5 = √5 / 5.</li><li>For binomial denominators:</li><li> 1 / (√3 + √2) × (√3 − √2)/(√3 − √2) = (√3 − √2)/(3 − 2) = √3 − √2.</li></ul><p><br></p><h4>3. MAGIC SHORTCUTS</h4><ol><li><strong>Comparison of Powers without Logarithm:</strong></li><li>If aˣ and bʸ are to be compared:</li><li>Take equal powers → Compare (aˣ)¹/ˣ and (bʸ)¹/ʸ.</li><li>Example: Which is greater: 3⁵ or 5³?</li><li>(3⁵)¹/⁵ = 3, (5³)¹/³ = 5. Since 5 &gt; 3 ⇒ 5³ &gt; 3⁵.</li><li><strong>Simplifying Nested Roots:</strong></li><li>√(a ± √b) can be written as √x ± √y, where:</li></ol><ul><li>x + y = a2√(xy) = √b</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Quick Estimation of Surds:</strong></li><li>√n ≈ k + (n − k²)/(2k), where k² is the nearest perfect square below n.</li><li>Example: √50 ≈ 7 + (50 − 49)/(2×7) = 7.07</li><li><strong>Identifying Perfect Squares Quickly:</strong></li></ol><ul><li>If last two digits ∈ {00, 01, 04, 09, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81}, number <em>may</em> be a perfect square.Else, it is <em>not</em> a perfect square.</li></ul><p><br></p><h4>4. ESSENTIAL FORMULAE &amp; METHODS</h4><p><strong>Laws of Indices:</strong></p><p>aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ</p><p>(aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ</p><p>a⁻ⁿ = 1 / aⁿ</p><p>a⁰ = 1</p><p><strong>Laws of Surds:</strong></p><p>√a × √b = √(ab)</p><p>√a / √b = √(a/b)</p><p>(√a)² = a</p><p><strong>Converting Surds to Rational Numbers:</strong></p><p>Multiply numerator and denominator by the <em>conjugate</em> of the denominator.</p><p>Example:</p><p>(5 + √3)/(2 + √3) × (2 − √3)/(2 − √3) = (5 + √3)(2 − √3) / (4 − 3) = (10 − 5√3 + 2√3 − 3) / 1 = 7 − 3√3</p><p><strong>Fractional Indices:</strong></p><p>a¹/² = √a, a¹/³ = ³√a</p><p>aᵐ/ⁿ = (ⁿ√a)ᵐ</p><p><br></p><h4>5. PROBLEM RECOGNITION &amp; CLASSIFICATION</h4><p><strong>Types of Problems:</strong></p><p><strong>Simplification Type:</strong></p><ul><li>Combine powers or surds using basic rules.</li><li>Example: Simplify (√3 + 1)².</li></ul><p><strong>Rationalization Type:</strong></p><ul><li>Eliminate surds from denominator.</li><li>Example: Rationalize 1 / (√5 − √2).</li></ul><p><strong>Comparison Type:</strong></p><ul><li>Compare expressions like 2³⁄⁵ and 3²⁄⁵.</li></ul><p><strong>Equation Type:</strong></p><ul><li>Solve equations with fractional powers: aˣ = b → x = logₐb.</li></ul><p><strong>Expression Simplification with Multiple Roots:</strong></p><ul><li>Simplify √(7 + 4√3), rewrite as (√3 + 2)² ⇒ 2 + √3.</li></ul><p><strong>Substitution Type:</strong></p><ul><li>Replace surds using algebraic identities to simplify complex roots.</li></ul><p><strong>Identification Keywords:</strong></p><ul><li>"Simplify", "Find value of", "Compare", "Rationalize", "Simplest form", "Evaluate".</li></ul><p><br></p><h4>6. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION METHODS</h4><p><strong>Model 1: Simplifying a Simple Surd Expression</strong></p><p>Example: Simplify √12 + √27.</p><p>Step 1: Break into perfect squares → √(4×3) + √(9×3)</p><p>Step 2: Simplify → 2√3 + 3√3</p><p>Step 3: Combine → 5√3</p><p><strong>Model 2: Rationalizing Denominator</strong></p><p>Example: 1 / (√2 + 1)</p><p>Step 1: Multiply numerator &amp; denominator by (√2 − 1)</p><p>Step 2: (1)(√2 − 1) / ((√2)² − 1²) = (√2 − 1)/(2 − 1) = √2 − 1</p><p><strong>Model 3: Simplifying with Indices</strong></p><p>Example: Simplify (2³ × 4⁻²) / (8⁻¹)</p><p>Step 1: Convert all to same base (2):</p><p>(2³ × (2²)⁻²) / (2³)⁻¹</p><p>= (2³ × 2⁻⁴) / 2⁻³</p><p>= 2⁻¹ / 2⁻³ = 2² = 4</p><p><strong style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">Model 4: Finding Value of Nested Surd</strong></p><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);"> Example: Find √(7 + 4√3)</span></p><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);"> Step 1: Assume = √a + √b</span></p><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);"> ⇒ a + b = 7, 2√(ab) = 4√3 ⇒ ab = 12</span></p><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);"> Step 2: Solve → a and b are 3 and 4</span></p><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);"> Hence √(7 + 4√3) = √3 + 2</span></p><p><br></p><h4>7. CONCLUSION</h4><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Surds retain their exact value — no rounding.</li><li>Always simplify roots into smallest integer × surd form.</li><li>Use laws of indices to simplify large power expressions quickly.</li><li>Rationalization helps eliminate irrational denominators — always multiply by the conjugate.</li></ul><p><strong>Speed &amp; Accuracy Tips:</strong></p><ul><li>Memorize perfect squares up to 30, cubes up to 15.</li><li>Practice converting all numbers into the same base for index problems.</li><li>Always check for hidden perfect squares or cubes within surds before simplifying.</li></ul><p><strong>Connections to Other Topics:</strong></p><ul><li><em>Indices</em> link directly to <em>Logarithms</em> (a future topic).</li><li><em>Surds</em> are often used in <em>Quadratic Equations</em> and <em>Simplifications</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Common Mistakes to Avoid</strong></p><ul><li>Forgetting to apply conjugate signs while rationalizing.</li><li>Mixing √(a + b) with √a + √b (which is incorrect).</li><li>Ignoring negative powers while simplifying indices.</li><li>Not expressing all bases in the same number before combining.</li></ul><p><br></p><h4><strong>Practice Question:</strong></h4><p>Simplify: $(\sqrt{5} - 1)^2 + 2\sqrt{5}$</p>',
    NULL, 'MEDIUM', 'CURATED', TRUE,
    CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP FROM DUAL
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE id = 'b3b3b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3b3b3b314');

-- 15. Profit & Loss -> topic b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b215
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, unit_id, name, description, difficulty, display_order, is_active, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT 'b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b215', '55555555-5555-5555-5555-555555555501',
    'b1b1b1b1-b1b1-b1b1-b1b1-b1b1b1b1b101', 'Profit & Loss',
    'Based on cost price, selling price, and calculating profit percentage or loss percentage', 'MEDIUM', 43, TRUE,
    CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP FROM DUAL
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM topics WHERE id = 'b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b215');
INSERT INTO lessons (id, topic_id, title, content, summary, difficulty, source_type, is_active, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT 'b3b3b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3b3b3b315', 'b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b215',
    'Profit & Loss Concepts',
    '<h3>1. CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATION</h3><p>The concept of <strong>Profit and Loss (P&amp;L)</strong> is a practical and essential application of percentage, ratio, and arithmetic. It is one of the most frequently tested topics in competitive exams and day-to-day financial reasoning.</p><p><strong>Definition:</strong></p><p>Every business transaction involves <strong>Cost Price (CP)</strong>, <strong>Selling Price (SP)</strong>, and possibly a <strong>Profit or Loss</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Profit (Gain):</strong> When Selling Price &gt; Cost Price</li><li><strong>Loss:</strong> When Selling Price &lt; Cost Price</li></ul><p><strong>Real-life Context:</strong></p><ul><li>Buying and selling goods</li><li>Retail pricing and discounts</li><li>Investment and return analysis</li><li>Business profit comparison</li></ul><p><strong>Mathematical Importance:</strong></p><p>P&amp;L integrates <strong>Percentages, Ratios, Averages</strong>, and <strong>Simple Interest</strong> principles, forming the base for advanced topics like <strong>Partnership</strong>, <strong>Compound Interest</strong>, and <strong>Data Interpretation</strong>.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h3>2. CORE THEORY &amp; PRINCIPLES</h3><p><strong>Key Definitions:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Cost Price (CP):</strong> The price at which an article is purchased.</li><li><strong>Selling Price (SP):</strong> The price at which the article is sold.</li><li><strong>Profit (Gain):</strong> SP − CP (if SP &gt; CP)</li><li><strong>Loss:</strong> CP − SP (if SP &lt; CP)</li><li><strong>Marked Price (MP):</strong> The price printed on the product (before discount).</li><li><strong>Discount:</strong> Reduction given on the marked price.</li></ol><p><strong>Core Relationships:</strong></p><ul><li>Profit% = (Profit / CP) × 100 = ((SP − CP) / CP) × 100</li><li>Loss% = (Loss / CP) × 100 = ((CP − SP) / CP) × 100</li><li>SP = CP × (1 + Profit%/100)</li><li>SP = CP × (1 − Loss%/100)</li><li>CP = SP × (100 / (100 + Profit%))</li><li>CP = SP × (100 / (100 − Loss%))</li></ul><p><strong>Principles:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Profit or Loss is always calculated on Cost Price (CP).</strong></li><li><strong>Discount is always calculated on Marked Price (MP).</strong></li><li><strong>When successive profits or losses occur</strong>, use the <em>successive percentage formula</em>.</li></ul><h3>3. MAGIC SHORTCUTS</h3><ol><li><strong>Direct Conversion Trick:</strong></li></ol><ul><li>SP = CP × (100 ± Profit or Loss%) / 100</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Successive Profit/Loss Shortcut:</strong></li><li>If a trader gains <em>a%</em> and then <em>b%</em>,</li><li>Net Gain% = a + b + (ab)/100</li><li>(Use “−b” for loss)</li><li><strong>Equal Profit and Loss Trick:</strong></li><li>If a trader gains <em>x%</em> on one article and loses <em>x%</em> on another of equal CP,</li><li>Then overall <strong>loss% = (x² / 100)</strong></li><li>Example: 20% gain on one, 20% loss on another → Overall loss = (20²/100) = 4%.</li><li><strong>Discount Equivalence Trick:</strong></li><li>Successive discounts of <em>a%</em> and <em>b%</em> are equivalent to one discount:</li><li>D = a + b − (ab)/100</li><li><strong>Marked Price Shortcut:</strong></li><li>MP = SP × 100 / (100 − Discount%)</li><li><strong>Profit on Selling Price Shortcut:</strong></li><li>If Profit% is given on SP,</li><li>Then Profit% on CP = (Profit% × 100) / (100 − Profit%)</li></ol><h3>4. ESSENTIAL FORMULAE &amp; METHODS</h3><p><strong>Basic Relations:</strong></p><ol><li>Profit = SP − CP</li><li>Loss = CP − SP</li><li>Profit% = (Profit / CP) × 100</li><li>Loss% = (Loss / CP) × 100</li></ol><p><strong>Reverse Relations:</strong></p><ol><li>CP = (100 × SP) / (100 + Profit%)</li><li>CP = (100 × SP) / (100 − Loss%)</li><li>SP = (100 + Profit%) × CP / 100</li><li>SP = (100 − Loss%) × CP / 100</li></ol><p><strong>Discount Relations:</strong></p><ol><li>Discount = MP − SP</li><li>Discount% = (Discount / MP) × 100</li><li>SP = MP × (100 − Discount%) / 100</li></ol><p><strong>Successive Discount:</strong></p><p>Equivalent Discount% = a + b − (ab)/100</p><p><strong>Combined Gain/Loss:</strong></p><p>When two items are sold at same SP,</p><p>Gain% = (Loss% / (100 − Loss%)) × 100</p><p><br></p><h3>5. PROBLEM RECOGNITION &amp; CLASSIFICATION</h3><table table_id="oybtplcaigh" id="oybtplcaigh" class=""><tr row_id="n9r34x497w" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="n9r34x497w" cell_id="pu1dstb4sgt" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p class="ql-align-center"><strong style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Type</strong></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="n9r34x497w" cell_id="kini1j1iv2a" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p class="ql-align-center"><strong style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Description</strong></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="n9r34x497w" cell_id="9nbhka4e96l" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p class="ql-align-center"><strong style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Key Words / Indicators</strong></p></td></tr><tr row_id="2je5jq0cb2v" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="2je5jq0cb2v" cell_id="icfzr8cmcu" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Type 1</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="2je5jq0cb2v" cell_id="0rdgq9ve8v1m" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Basic profit/loss computation</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="2je5jq0cb2v" cell_id="5vvhu8q8pc" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">“Bought for… sold for…”</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="2qxt9s4tp9" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="2qxt9s4tp9" cell_id="3i7rrin9fsh" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Type 2</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="2qxt9s4tp9" cell_id="36wfh54w0r5" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Finding CP/SP from %</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="2qxt9s4tp9" cell_id="7rbqcd3yux5" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">“Find cost price when profit 20% and SP given”</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="vvqo8kek8kc" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="vvqo8kek8kc" cell_id="ivxpcigoeai" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Type 3</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="vvqo8kek8kc" cell_id="npjcsso1jqb" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Successive Profit/Loss</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="vvqo8kek8kc" cell_id="uaanwtu2ihj" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">“Gains 20% and 10% successively”</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="b8t2yfypxr8" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="b8t2yfypxr8" cell_id="g5470muynv" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Type 4</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="b8t2yfypxr8" cell_id="t50ecwg9gdn" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Equal CP or Equal SP models</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="b8t2yfypxr8" cell_id="o5fiw12zw8j" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">“Two articles bought/sold at same CP or SP”</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="ae8r2s4o1gw" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="ae8r2s4o1gw" cell_id="a520qurlhao" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Type 5</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="ae8r2s4o1gw" cell_id="gdsazyt4j6a" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Discount-based</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="ae8r2s4o1gw" cell_id="vkovi70kthn" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">“Shopkeeper allows 10% discount on MP”</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="evwor0ae99" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="evwor0ae99" cell_id="b613m47l64e" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Type 6</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="evwor0ae99" cell_id="kn3aqlwf2f" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Marked Price &amp; Discount</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="evwor0ae99" cell_id="0fj5h6wfu5hl" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">“Find MP when given SP and discount”</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="zdf62uoanwn" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="zdf62uoanwn" cell_id="fky9h5mad7h" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Type 7</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="zdf62uoanwn" cell_id="3ennegkyer" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">False Weight / Adulteration</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="zdf62uoanwn" cell_id="plzxntiffa" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">“Sells at cost price but cheats in weight”</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="jsghg3b7r2g" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="jsghg3b7r2g" cell_id="a5bxaypovdw" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Type 8</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="jsghg3b7r2g" cell_id="pe87fuvsuas" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Overall Profit &amp; Loss</span></p></td><td table_id="oybtplcaigh" row_id="jsghg3b7r2g" cell_id="9mknxw4xevb" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">“Buys several articles… sells all together…”</span></p></td></tr></table><p><br></p><p><br></p><h3>6. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION METHODS</h3><p><br></p><p><strong>Model 1: Finding SP or CP</strong></p><p>Find SP of article costing ₹250 at a gain of 20%.</p><p>SP = CP × (1 + 20/100) = 250 × 1.2 = ₹300.</p><p>Find CP of article sold at ₹480 with 20% gain.</p><p>CP = (480 × 100) / (100 + 20) = ₹400.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Model 2: Successive Profit/Loss</strong></p><p>Gain 10% then 20%.</p><p>Net gain% = 10 + 20 + (10×20)/100 = 32%.</p><p><strong>Model 3: Equal Gain &amp; Loss</strong></p><p>Gain 10% on one item, lose 10% on another (same CP).</p><p>Net loss% = (10² / 100) = 1%.</p><p><strong>Model 4: Discount Problem</strong></p><p>MP = ₹800, discount 10%, find SP.</p><p>SP = 800 × (100 − 10)/100 = ₹720.</p><p><strong>Model 5: False Weight</strong></p><p>If trader sells goods at cost price but uses 900g in place of 1kg:</p><p>Profit% = (1000 − 900)/900 × 100 = 11.11%.</p><p><strong>Model 6: Combination Profit/Loss</strong></p><p>If total CP = ₹1,000 and total SP = ₹1,200 → Profit% = (200/1000)×100 = 20%.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h3>7. CONCLUSION</h3><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Always calculate profit/loss on <em>Cost Price</em>.</li><li>Discounts are based on <em>Marked Price</em>.</li><li>In successive gains/losses, use combination formula (a + b + ab/100).</li><li>Equal profit–loss model always results in a <strong>net loss</strong>.</li><li>False weight and adulteration questions are disguised profit models.</li></ul><p><strong>Speed Tips:</strong></p><ul><li>Convert all values to % form for faster calculation.</li><li>Memorize equivalent fraction–percent pairs (20% = 1/5, 25% = 1/4).</li><li>For multiple items, find total CP and SP, then compare directly.</li><li>Use net % formula to avoid stepwise calculations in successive cases.</li></ul><p><strong>Practical Use:</strong></p><p>Mastering Profit &amp; Loss helps in understanding real-world business models, pricing, and is foundational for higher financial topics like <strong>Simple Interest</strong>, <strong>Partnership</strong>, and <strong>Data Interpretation</strong>.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h3>8. COMMON MISTAKES TO AVOID</h3><ol><li>Calculating profit% on SP instead of CP.</li><li>Ignoring difference between <em>Discount</em> and <em>Loss</em>.</li><li>Adding successive profits directly instead of using net % formula.</li><li>Mixing up “equal CP” and “equal SP” models.</li><li>Failing to check if SP or CP is the base in mixed questions.</li></ol><p><br></p><p><strong>Practice Question:</strong></p><p>A pen is bought for ₹400 and sold for ₹500. Find profit%.</p>',
    NULL, 'MEDIUM', 'CURATED', TRUE,
    CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP FROM DUAL
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE id = 'b3b3b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3b3b3b315');

-- 16. Time & Distance -> topic b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b216
