SELECT 'b3b3b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3b3b3b321', 'b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b221',
    'Chain Rule Concepts',
    '<h3>1. CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATION</h3><p>The <strong>Chain Rule</strong> is used to solve problems where <strong>two or more quantities are related in a proportional manner</strong>.</p><p>It helps determine how the change in one quantity affects another when several variables are interdependent.</p><p><br></p><h4>Real-World Context</h4><ul><li>Time taken to complete a task when the number of workers changes.</li><li>Speed, distance, and time-based relationships.</li><li>Production, consumption, or efficiency-related problems.</li></ul><h4>Mathematical Significance</h4><p>The Chain Rule combines <strong>direct</strong> and <strong>inverse proportions</strong> to form multi-step proportional relationships.</p><h4>Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Understanding of <strong>ratios and proportions</strong></li><li>Knowledge of <strong>unitary method</strong></li><li>Familiarity with <strong>inverse and direct proportion concepts</strong></li></ul><p><br></p><h3>2. CORE THEORY &amp; PRINCIPLES</h3><h4>2.1 Fundamental Idea</h4><p>If several quantities are <strong>proportionally related</strong>, we use the <strong>Chain Rule</strong> to connect them systematically.</p><p>The rule helps compare multiple variable conditions such as:</p><ul><li>More men, less time</li><li>More speed, less time</li><li>More machines, more work</li></ul><h4>2.2 Types of Proportion</h4><ol><li><strong>Direct Proportion:</strong></li><li>When increase in one quantity leads to an increase in another.</li><li>$\text{Example:} \quad \text{Work} \propto \text{Number of Men}$</li><li><strong>Inverse Proportion:</strong></li><li>When increase in one quantity causes decrease in another.</li><li>$\text{Example:} \quad \text{Time} \propto \frac{1}{\text{Number of Men}}$</li></ol><h4>2.3 The General Relationship</h4><p>If a quantity depends on multiple factors, such as men, days, and hours:</p><p>$\text{Result} \propto \frac{M_1 \times D_1 \times H_1}{M_2 \times D_2 \times H_2}$</p><p>The Chain Rule helps find the missing variable systematically by chaining all related ratios.</p><p><br></p><h3>3. MAGIC SHORTCUTS</h3><ol><li><strong>Shortcut 1: Direct Relation</strong></li></ol><ul><li>“More → More” relation</li><li> $\Rightarrow$ Multiply directly.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Shortcut 2: Inverse Relation</strong></li></ol><ul><li>“More → Less” relation</li><li> $\Rightarrow$ Multiply inversely (swap numerator and denominator).</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Shortcut 3: Chain of Ratios</strong></li></ol><ul><li>Always link variables in order using ratio comparison.</li><li> $\text{Required Quantity} = \text{Known Quantity} \times \frac{\text{Product of Related Terms}}{\text{Product of Opposite Terms}}$</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Shortcut 4: Single Step Conversion</strong></li><li>Convert any complex proportional problem into a single fraction and simplify.</li></ol><p><br></p><h3>4. ESSENTIAL FORMULAE &amp; METHODS</h3><table table_id="ijzetpjuv5l" id="ijzetpjuv5l" class=""><tr row_id="cy0v46ohsnm" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="ijzetpjuv5l" row_id="cy0v46ohsnm" cell_id="p0n2cbqc2xn" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p class="ql-align-center"><strong style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Concept</strong></p></td><td table_id="ijzetpjuv5l" row_id="cy0v46ohsnm" cell_id="vbp8c7tib6d" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p class="ql-align-center"><strong style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Formula</strong></p></td></tr><tr row_id="bsblahao51i" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="ijzetpjuv5l" row_id="bsblahao51i" cell_id="47kdgm0bzz4" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Direct proportion</span></p></td><td table_id="ijzetpjuv5l" row_id="bsblahao51i" cell_id="64sltkbrjay" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">$\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \frac{b_1}{b_2}$</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="gabjw9qgodk" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="ijzetpjuv5l" row_id="gabjw9qgodk" cell_id="428p7w6mfi8" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Inverse proportion</span></p></td><td table_id="ijzetpjuv5l" row_id="gabjw9qgodk" cell_id="czpjpd46prw" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">$\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \frac{b_2}{b_1}$</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="0fz97kjjn13f" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="ijzetpjuv5l" row_id="0fz97kjjn13f" cell_id="4ybtyr1qzmt" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Combined Chain Rule</span></p></td><td table_id="ijzetpjuv5l" row_id="0fz97kjjn13f" cell_id="e3dhhwa1dn7" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">$\frac{x_1}{x_2} = \frac{a_1 \times b_1 \times c_1}{a_2 \times b_2 \times c_2}$</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="v52mtpdzx2" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="ijzetpjuv5l" row_id="v52mtpdzx2" cell_id="yxyz2afoq3s" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Work relation</span></p></td><td table_id="ijzetpjuv5l" row_id="v52mtpdzx2" cell_id="am5fvy5sh9" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">$M_1 D_1 H_1 W_1 = M_2 D_2 H_2 W_2$</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="ojqyf98epk" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="ijzetpjuv5l" row_id="ojqyf98epk" cell_id="m905chztamp" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Unit Work</span></p></td><td table_id="ijzetpjuv5l" row_id="ojqyf98epk" cell_id="li4nhxlk6w" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">$\text{Work done} = \text{Men} \times \text{Days} \times \text{Hours}$</span></p></td></tr></table><p><br></p><h3>5. PROBLEM RECOGNITION &amp; CLASSIFICATION</h3><p><strong>Type 1: Simple Direct Proportion</strong></p><p>Problems involving “more-more” or “less-less” relationships.</p><p>Example: “If 5 men complete a job in 12 days, how long will 10 men take?”</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Type 2: Simple Inverse Proportion</strong></p><p>Problems involving “more-less” relationships.</p><p>Example: “If 8 men take 15 days, how long will 12 men take?”</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Type 3: Multi-variable Chain Problems</strong></p><p>Problems involving multiple changing factors like men, days, hours, work done, or efficiency.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Type 4: Mixed Relation (Direct + Inverse)</strong></p><p>When some factors increase and others decrease simultaneously.</p><p>Example: “If 10 men working 8 hours a day complete a work in 15 days, in how many days will 20 men working 10 hours a day complete it?”</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Type 5: Real-life Applications</strong></p><p>Includes questions on speed-time-distance, production, wages, and consumption.</p><p><br></p><h3>6. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION METHODS</h3><h4>Example 1: Direct Proportion</h4><p><strong>Question:</strong></p><p>If 8 men can complete a job in 12 days, in how many days can 16 men do it?</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong></p><p>As men increase, time decreases ⇒ <strong>Inverse Proportion</strong></p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong></p><p>$M_1 D_1 = M_2 D_2$</p><p>$\Rightarrow 8 \times 12 = 16 \times D_2$</p><p>$\Rightarrow D_2 = \frac{8 \times 12}{16} = 6$</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong></p><p>16 men can complete the job in <strong>6 days</strong>.</p><p><br></p><h4>Example 2: Multi-variable Chain Rule</h4><p><strong>Question:</strong></p><p>12 men can complete a work in 15 days, working 8 hours a day.</p><p>How many days will 18 men take, working 10 hours a day?</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong></p><p>Let required days = $D_2$.</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong></p><p>Apply Chain Rule formula:</p><p>$\frac{D_2}{15} = \frac{12 \times 8}{18 \times 10}$</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong></p><p>$D_2 = 15 \times \frac{12 \times 8}{18 \times 10}$</p><p>$\Rightarrow D_2 = 8$</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong></p><p>18 men working 10 hours/day will complete the work in <strong>8 days</strong>.</p><p><br></p><h4>Example 3: Mixed Relation</h4><p><strong>Question:</strong></p><p>If 6 men or 8 women can do a piece of work in 12 days, how long will 3 men and 4 women take?</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong></p><p>Let total work = $W$.</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong></p><p>$6M \times 12 = 8W \times 12$</p><p>1 man’s 1 day work = $\frac{1}{72}$</p><p>1 woman’s 1 day work = $\frac{1}{96}$</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong></p><p>Work done by 3 men and 4 women in one day = $3 \times \frac{1}{72} + 4 \times \frac{1}{96}$</p><p>$= \frac{1}{24}$</p><p><strong>Step 4:</strong></p><p>Therefore, total days = $24$.</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong></p><p>3 men and 4 women will complete the work in <strong>24 days</strong>.</p><p><br></p><h4>Example 4: Speed and Distance</h4><p><strong>Question:</strong></p><p>If a car travels 120 km in 3 hours, how long will it take to travel 200 km at the same speed?</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p>Time $\propto$ Distance</p><p>$\Rightarrow \frac{T_1}{T_2} = \frac{D_1}{D_2}$</p><p>$\Rightarrow \frac{3}{T_2} = \frac{120}{200}$</p><p>$\Rightarrow T_2 = 5$ hours.</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong></p><p>The car will take <strong>5 hours</strong>.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h3>7. CONCLUSION</h3><p>The <strong>Chain Rule</strong> is a powerful method that connects multiple proportional relationships into one equation.</p><p>It generalizes <strong>ratio and proportion</strong> concepts to handle real-life quantitative problems efficiently.</p><p><br></p><h4>Key Insights</h4><ul><li>Identify <strong>direct</strong> or <strong>inverse</strong> relationships first.</li><li>Convert every statement into a proportional equation.</li><li>Multiply directly for <strong>more-more</strong> or <strong>less-less</strong>, and inversely for <strong>more-less</strong> relations.</li><li>The formula $M_1 D_1 H_1 W_1 = M_2 D_2 H_2 W_2$ covers all types of work-related problems.</li></ul><h4>Mastery Indicators</h4><ul><li>Able to classify problems as direct or inverse proportion.</li><li>Can chain multiple proportional factors correctly.</li><li>Solves standard problems in 1–2 minutes with complete accuracy.</li></ul><p><br></p><p>Practice Questions:</p><p>12 men working 8 hours/day finish a work in 10 days. In how many days will 16 men working 6 hours/day finish the same work (same efficiency)?</p>',
    NULL, 'MEDIUM', 'CURATED', TRUE,
    CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP FROM DUAL
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE id = 'b3b3b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3b3b3b321');

-- 22. True Discount -> topic b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b222
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, unit_id, name, description, difficulty, display_order, is_active, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT 'b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b222', '55555555-5555-5555-5555-555555555501',
    'b1b1b1b1-b1b1-b1b1-b1b1-b1b1b1b1b101', 'True Discount',
    'Calculates reduction allowed on a future payment made before its due date', 'MEDIUM', 50, TRUE,
    CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP FROM DUAL
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM topics WHERE id = 'b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b222');
INSERT INTO lessons (id, topic_id, title, content, summary, difficulty, source_type, is_active, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT 'b3b3b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3b3b3b322', 'b2b2b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2-b2b2b2b2b222',
    'True Discount Concepts',
    '<h3>1. CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATION</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong></p><p>The True Discount (TD) is the <strong>difference between the present worth (PW)</strong> of a sum of money due at a future date and its <strong>nominal (future) value (FV)</strong>.</p><p>It represents the <strong>loss</strong> a person suffers by receiving the payment before the due date.</p><p>If a person is to receive a sum of money after some time but receives it <strong>in advance</strong>, the true discount is the <strong>difference between the amount due and the amount received now</strong>.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h4>Real-World Context</h4><p>True Discount problems occur in:</p><ul><li>Commercial transactions involving credit or bills of exchange.</li><li>Banking and financial calculations where early payments are made.</li><li>Discounting of promissory notes or future receivables.</li></ul><h4>Mathematical Significance</h4><p>True Discount builds on <strong>Simple Interest</strong> and <strong>Present Value</strong> concepts.</p><p>It models <strong>time-value of money</strong> — understanding how much future money is worth today.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h4>Prerequisites</h4><p>Before learning True Discount, students should understand:</p><ul><li><strong>Simple Interest formula:</strong> $SI = \frac{P \times R \times T}{100}$</li><li><strong>Basic percentage and ratio</strong> operations.</li><li><strong>Concept of present worth</strong> (money equivalence at different times).</li></ul><p><br></p><p><br></p><h3>2. CORE THEORY &amp; PRINCIPLES</h3><h4>2.1 Key Concepts</h4><ol><li><strong>Nominal Value (Face Value):</strong> The amount due at the end of a certain period.</li><li><strong>Present Worth (PW):</strong> The current value of the amount due after discounting interest for the remaining time.</li><li><strong>True Discount (TD):</strong> The difference between nominal value and present worth.</li></ol><p>Hence,</p><p>$\text{True Discount} = \text{Nominal Value} - \text{Present Worth}$</p><p>and</p><p>$\text{Simple Interest on Present Worth for the unexpired time} = \text{True Discount}$</p><p><br></p><h4>2.2 Relationship Between Terms</h4><p>Let:</p><ul><li>Face Value (Amount due) = $A$</li><li>Present Worth = $PW$</li><li>Rate of Interest = $R%$ per annum</li><li>Time = $T$ years</li><li>Then,</li></ul><p>$\text{True Discount} = A - PW$</p><p>and</p><p>$\text{Simple Interest on PW} = TD$</p><p>Thus,</p><p>$\frac{PW \times R \times T}{100} = TD$</p><p>From this,</p><p>$PW = \frac{A \times 100}{100 + R \times T}$</p><p>and</p><p>$TD = \frac{A \times R \times T}{100 + R \times T}$</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h3>3. MAGIC SHORTCUTS</h3><ol><li><strong>True Discount Formula (Direct):</strong></li><li>$TD = \frac{A \times R \times T}{100 + R \times T}$</li><li><strong>Present Worth Formula:</strong></li><li>$PW = \frac{A \times 100}{100 + R \times T}$</li><li><strong>Simple Interest Relation:</strong></li><li>$SI - TD = \frac{TD^2}{A}$</li><li><strong>When Rate and Time Are Small:</strong></li><li>Approximate True Discount $\approx \frac{A \times R \times T}{100}$</li><li>(same as Simple Interest).</li><li><strong>Double-Check Trick:</strong></li><li>$PW + TD = A$ (always holds true).</li></ol><p><br></p><p><br></p><h3>4. ESSENTIAL FORMULAE &amp; METHODS</h3><table table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" id="8jahfe0q8zc" class=""><tr row_id="awbqagca4dn" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="awbqagca4dn" cell_id="dvg7jihfmij" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p class="ql-align-center"><strong style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">Concept</strong></p></td><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="awbqagca4dn" cell_id="h2whjc9dfrf" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p class="ql-align-center"><strong style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">Formula</strong></p></td><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="awbqagca4dn" cell_id="sio3vioozha" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p class="ql-align-center"><strong style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">Description</strong></p></td></tr><tr row_id="yfkareuekqe" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="yfkareuekqe" cell_id="1f9qzgb3m8c" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">Present Worth</span></p></td><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="yfkareuekqe" cell_id="bjpo70dwyav" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">$PW = \frac{A \times 100}{100 + R \times T}$</span></p></td><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="yfkareuekqe" cell_id="8s0tf3efkpk" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">Current equivalent of future amount</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="rryb6xlgcrc" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="rryb6xlgcrc" cell_id="g9tvtwzsq8s" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">True Discount</span></p></td><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="rryb6xlgcrc" cell_id="t99lo3xxacf" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">$TD = \frac{A \times R \times T}{100 + R \times T}$</span></p></td><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="rryb6xlgcrc" cell_id="cykjed7fu2m" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">Difference between face value and present worth</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="5gctybigrg3" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="5gctybigrg3" cell_id="h6oxczqsb2t" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">Relationship</span></p></td><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="5gctybigrg3" cell_id="5uj2tuub51b" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">$SI - TD = \frac{TD^2}{A}$</span></p></td><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="5gctybigrg3" cell_id="8netooeo488" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">Link between Simple Interest and True Discount</span></p></td></tr><tr row_id="fuz2acuis5t" style="width: 100%;"><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="fuz2acuis5t" cell_id="dh93x7qg4qk" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">When TD = SI</span></p></td><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="fuz2acuis5t" cell_id="aydljtdobye" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">$A = 2 \times PW$</span></p></td><td table_id="8jahfe0q8zc" row_id="fuz2acuis5t" cell_id="fmpx8p7taqp" column="null" color="null" style="background-color: null" colspan="null"><p><span style="background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">Happens when amount is twice the present worth</span></p></td></tr></table><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h3>5. PROBLEM RECOGNITION &amp; CLASSIFICATION</h3><p><strong>Type 1: Basic TD Calculation</strong></p><p>Find the True Discount given Amount, Rate, and Time.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Type 2: Present Worth Calculation</strong></p><p>Find the Present Worth when TD, Rate, and Time are given.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Type 3: Rate or Time Finding</strong></p><p>Find Rate of Interest or Time when TD and other values are given.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Type 4: Relation between SI and TD</strong></p><p>Find difference or ratio between Simple Interest and True Discount.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Type 5: Commercial Scenarios</strong></p><p>Early payments, discounted bills, and bank transactions based on TD.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h3>6. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION METHODS</h3><h4>Example 1: Basic TD Calculation</h4><p><strong>Question:</strong></p><p>Find the True Discount on Rs. 2600 due 2 years hence at 10% per annum simple interest.</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p>Use $TD = \frac{A \times R \times T}{100 + R \times T}$</p><p>$TD = \frac{2600 \times 10 \times 2}{100 + 10 \times 2}$</p><p>$TD = \frac{52000}{120} = 433.33$</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> True Discount = Rs. 433.33</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h4>Example 2: Finding Present Worth</h4><p><strong>Question:</strong></p><p>Find the Present Worth of Rs. 3000 due 2 years hence at 5% simple interest.</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p>$PW = \frac{A \times 100}{100 + R \times T}$</p><p>$PW = \frac{3000 \times 100}{100 + 5 \times 2}$</p><p>$PW = \frac{300000}{110} = 2727.27$</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Present Worth = Rs. 2727.27</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h4>Example 3: Relation between SI and TD</h4><p><strong>Question:</strong></p><p>If the Simple Interest on a sum for a certain time and rate is Rs. 90 and the True Discount on the same sum and time is Rs. 81, find the sum.</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p>Use $SI - TD = \frac{TD^2}{A}$</p><p>$90 - 81 = \frac{81^2}{A}$</p><p>$9 = \frac{6561}{A}$</p><p>$A = \frac{6561}{9} = 729$</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Amount = Rs. 729</p><p><br></p><h4>Example 4: Finding Time</h4><p><strong>Question:</strong></p><p>A sum of Rs. 2000 is due in certain time. If the true discount is Rs. 200 at 10% per annum, find the time.</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p>Use $TD = \frac{A \times R \times T}{100 + R \times T}$</p><p>$\Rightarrow 200 = \frac{2000 \times 10 \times T}{100 + 10T}$</p><p>$200(100 + 10T) = 20000T$</p><p>$20000 + 2000T = 20000T$</p><p>$20000 = 18000T$</p><p>$T = \frac{20000}{18000} = 1.11$ years</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Time = 1.11 years (≈ 1 year 1 month 10 days)</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><h3>7. CONCLUSION</h3><p><strong>True Discount</strong> represents the <strong>real financial advantage or loss</strong> when a payment is made before the due date.</p><p>It helps understand <strong>present worth</strong> and <strong>future value</strong> under simple interest.</p><p><br></p><h4><strong>Key Takeaways</strong></h4><ul><li>True Discount is <strong>always less than Simple Interest</strong> for the same amount, rate, and time.</li><li>Present Worth and True Discount maintain a constant relation:</li><li>$PW + TD = A$</li><li>Essential for commerce, trade, and bank-related aptitude problems.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><br></p><p>Practice Question:</p><p>Find the True Discount (TD) and Present Worth (PW) on a sum of ₹2400 due after 2 years at 10% p.a. simple interest.</p>',
    NULL, 'MEDIUM', 'CURATED', TRUE,
    CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP FROM DUAL
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE id = 'b3b3b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3-b3b3b3b3b322');

